      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 The Authors. Production and hosting by Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Lysophosphatidic acid (LPA) initiates demyelination following peripheral nerve injury, which causes neuropathic pain. Our previous in vivo and ex vivo studies using mice have demonstrated that LPA-induced demyelination of spinal dorsal roots is attributed by the LPA1-type receptor-mediated down-regulation of myelin-related molecules, such as MBP and MPZ. In this study using S16 mature-type Schwann cells, we found that LPA-induced down-regulation of myelin-related genes is attributed by the activation of LPA1 receptor, Rho kinase, and p300, leading to an acetylation of NFκB, which down-regulates the transcription of Sox10, MBP and MPZ genes."
      },
